  "worked_solution": "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\ne -2.$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2x+4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{(2x)(2x+4)-2{x}^{2}}{(2x+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x(x+4)}{(2x+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x}{(2x+4)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\frac{(4x+8)(2x+4)^{2}-4(2{x}^{2}+8x)(2x+4)}{(2x+4)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(x+2)^{2}-8{x}^{2}-32x}{(2x+4)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{x}^{2}+32x+32-8{x}^{2}-32x}{(2x+4)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}} \\\\  & =4(x+2)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x+2)^{-3} & >0 \\\\ (x+2)^{-3} & >0 \\\\ x+2 & >0 \\\\ x & >-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\,-2,\\infty \\,).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
